# Deep Review — 2026-06-09

Multi-agent review: 7 dimensions (spine correctness, infra correctness, security, concurrency/durability, architecture, docs drift, tests, prototype hygiene), every bug-class finding verified by two independent adversarial agents, plus a completeness critic. 89 agents total.

## Dimension summaries

### correctness-spine

The core spine is unusually disciplined — hash-pinned artifacts, schema-parsed boundaries, and a safety-envelope machine whose transitions line up exactly with how WorkflowApp.run drives it (I traced every send against machine.ts and found no silent no-op transitions). However, I found and empirically proved one real hole in the central guarantee: a generated XState machine whose initial state is the 'done' final state passes every validator and yields a fully 'captured' run receipt with zero steps executed (verified by running the actual WorkflowApp with a wrapped planner: status=captured, completedStepIds=[]). Two systemic issues compound it: unhandled adapter throws escape the safety envelope leaving the D1 projection/event log permanently non-terminal, and capability lease IDs are keyed by capability+runId so duplicate same-capability steps break differently in prod (D1 PK violation throw) than in tests (silent receipt overwrite). The workflow-nodes and cartridge layers (dream-memory-fabric, artifact-backed adapter, dream proof recorder) are clean — consistent blocked-result plumbing, correct primary-output-ref ordering, and the node-type dispatch fallthrough is exhaustive against the enum.

### correctness-infra

The infrastructure adapters are mostly carefully built — D1 schema/migrations match the SQL in the event stream, status projection, review gate, seeder, and telemetry sinks, and the GitHub/Linear/Wzrrd adapters do defensive content-hash verification. The serious problems are: the Discord path is the one capability whose pinned content is never actually hash-verified (the body can be swapped post-approval by anything with the run-repo write token, which the agent lanes hold); the capability lease broker is the only non-idempotent D1 writer (deterministic lease/receipt IDs + plain INSERT means same-capability duplicate steps or run retries die on UNIQUE constraints mid-run); and the production "durable workflow" path is not durable at all — the whole multi-lane run executes inside one Worker fetch invocation with no Workflows binding. There is also a verified zod bug that makes package discovery throw for any NULL manifest_hash row, and the Analytics Engine sink ignores the platform's 5120-byte blob and 25-points-per-invocation limits.

### security

The production hexagonal app has a serious authentication gap: the expensive, side-effect-producing POST /runs route (and the /runs/:runId event/debugger read routes) has no auth at all, while the cheap admin/package-seed setup routes are correctly protected by a timing-safe admin token. The deployed Worker is public, so this is the dominant risk. Secret handling in package.json scripts and the extract-codex-auth/wrangler-token helpers is reasonable (piped stdin, base64, no argv/file leakage), and the trusted local dream-memory relay defaults to 127.0.0.1 with a required bearer token. Sandbox command construction passes user/workflow input through the env map rather than interpolating into shell strings, so I found no shell-injection vector; .env/.dev.vars are gitignored and committed artifacts (.wzrrd/state.json, migrations) contain no secrets. Secondary issues: one prototype is open-by-default, several accept the token via URL query param, and the per-run Artifacts write token is exposed inside the agent sandbox.

### concurrency-durability

The production spine has strong artifact-pinning/hash hygiene but its concurrency-and-durability story is structurally hollow: the entire multi-lane run executes inside a single HTTP fetch with an in-memory XState actor (nothing survives a restart), the lane admission DO has no expiry/alarm so a single crash permanently leaks concurrency slots and wedges the work item, and the capability lease ledger is write-only — expiry and single-use status exist in D1 but are never read or enforced, while deterministic lease/lane IDs make any same-runId retry hard-fail after side effects already fired. The queue-consuming prototypes show the same family of bugs at different maturity: the parallel spike double-admits running lanes on redelivery and wedges forever on any finalization crash (a bug the newer swarm spike fixed but never backported), and the registry spike re-runs terminal jobs with no idempotency. No reaper, timeout, or resume mechanism exists anywhere in src/app (zero alarm() usage), so every crash window identified ends in a permanently stuck run rather than a recovery.

### architecture

The hexagon is structurally real: domain and application have zero Cloudflare imports, infrastructure adapters depend inward through ports.ts, prototypes are correctly firewalled (no src/ <-> prototypes/ imports), and the machine.ts vs generated-machine.ts split is sound — the generated machine document is produced at runtime, executed via createGeneratedWorkflowActor, hash-pinned (machine.config.json + rendered machine.ts source), and re-verified on reload (sha256 check at workflow-app.ts:2946) plus in dream-generated-workflow-proof. The real architectural debt is depth, not direction: capability support is copy-pasted monomorphically across all layers (five identical adapter interfaces, five identical broker record methods, four ~200-line delivery pipelines), workflow-app.ts is a 4,241-line god-file that embeds domain validation and its own integration-test doubles, and two boundary leaks undercut the spine — infrastructure imports src/cartridges (plugins wired inside the spine instead of the composition root) and AgentLaneRuntimeRequest hardcodes the sandbox script's filesystem/git/provider mechanics into a port. Highest leverage: genericize the capability pipeline, split workflow-app, and invert the cartridge registry to the worker entrypoint.

### docs-drift

The docs are unusually receipt-dense and most concrete claims check out: every pnpm command cited in README/docs/dogfood-plan exists in package.json, all referenced files/prototypes/migrations exist, the D1 table list matches code, the 156-test integration suite passes, and the renamed demo->proof scripts left no stale references. The real drift is concentrated in two places: a large undocumented production surface (the dream-memory-fabric/cartridge/workflow-nodes subsystem, ~4,200 lines plus scripts and 11 tests, invisible in every doc including CONTEXT.md's "one production context" claim), and workflow-app-spine.md describing the generated machine source and harness as optional when the schema and loader make both mandatory and execution-blocking. Smaller staleness: production-vs-prototypes.md still says the capsule DO is not deployed as the production Worker path (it is), the documented step-kind and safety-envelope state lists each omit one real entry, README understates the route surface, and the prototypes index is missing six spikes.

### tests

The suite is in good shape mechanically — 156/156 pass in 678ms, typecheck clean, zero skips, near-universal toStrictEqual on full composite objects (only 2 loose assertions in 16k lines), and denial/blocker paths (review_required, payload_hash_mismatch, planner failures, protocol violations) are genuinely asserted at the application layer. But it is an application-layer suite wearing an integration-test label: nothing touches workerd, real D1, Durable Object storage, a Cloudflare Sandbox, or a git remote, so the three components the repo exists for — the Sandbox Pi lane runtime, the concurrency-controlling capsule supervisor DO, and the Artifacts git store I/O — have zero coverage, and every D1/admission interaction is a hand-rolled fake that can drift from real behavior. The sharpest gaps a maintainer should act on: POST /runs has no auth and no test demanding any, and the production-mode safety guards (real-agent enforcement, verifier requirement, Cloudflare execution proof) are never asserted — the one production-mode test passes in Node because the 'Cloudflare execution proof' is a hardcoded constant.

### prototypes-hygiene

Mechanical hygiene is mostly clean: the run-real-demo.mjs -> run-real-proof.mjs renames left zero stale references, every package.json prototype/app script target exists on disk, src/ contains no imports from prototypes/, and prototypes/_captures/ is an empty untracked directory (delete it, but it breaks nothing). The real problem is graduation debt: at least 7 prototypes whose own READMEs say 'delete after rewritten into src/' have had that condition fire (capsule supervisor DO, capability lease broker, review gate actor, package registry, planner lane all now live in src/app and wrangler.jsonc) yet remain 'Status: active', several with deployed Workers, queues, and real secrets (GitHub App private key). Supporting drift compounds it: docs/production-vs-prototypes.md still claims shipped seams are 'not yet' in production, prototypes/README.md omits 6 of 16 prototypes, three copy-pasted SvelteKit report UIs have already diverged, and untracked junk (.wzrrd/ machine-local publish state, shitrat-brain-proposal-ui's un-ignored build/.svelte-kit) is one 'git add .' away from being committed.

## Confirmed findings

### [CRITICAL] [concurrency-durability] Entire production workflow run executes inside one HTTP fetch invocation — no durable execution, no resume path

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-worker-route.ts:1311`

POST /runs synchronously awaits the whole multi-lane run (planner Pi lane, N worker Pi lanes, verifier Pi lane, capability deliveries) inside the fetch handler. The safety-envelope state machine is an in-memory XState actor created per request (workflow-app.ts:1238 'const actor = createActor(dynamicWorkflowSafetyEnvelopeMachine)'); nothing is checkpointed for resumption. There is no Cloudflare Workflow, no queue, no waitUntil, and zero alarm() usage anywhere in src/app (verified by grep). If the worker is evicted, hits limits, or the isolate dies mid-run (these runs span multiple multi-minute sandbox lanes), the run dies silently: the D1 runs row stays frozen at its last state forever (no reaper marks it failed), admitted lanes stay 'active' in the supervisor DO forever, and the issued capability lease rows stay 'issued' forever. This directly contradicts the dimension requirement that workflow state machines survive restarts — the production spine does not survive a restart at all.

**Evidence:** cloudflare-worker-route.ts:1308-1315: `const result = WorkflowRunResultSchema.parse(await frontDoor.startRun(body)); return Response.json(result);` — and front-door startRun (cloudflare-workflow-front-door.ts:590) is `return await workflow.run(request);` with no step durability. workflow-app.ts:1237-1241: `const eventLog: WorkflowEvent[] = []; const actor = createActor(dynamicWorkflowSafetyEnvelopeMachine); ... actor.start();`

**Fix:** Move run execution onto Cloudflare Workflows (or a queue + DO state machine where each transition is persisted and resumable), and add a reaper (DO alarm or cron) that times out runs stuck in non-terminal states in D1.

### [CRITICAL] [security] Production POST /runs triggers paid agent runs and real side-effects with zero authentication

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-worker-route.ts:1262`

The deployed Worker's default export is createWorkflowWorkerHandler() (src/app/worker.ts:9) with no auth wrapper. handleWorkflowWorkerRequest gates /admin/packages/* behind requirePackageSeedAuth (WORKFLOW_APP_ADMIN_TOKEN, timing-safe), but the /runs route goes straight from schema validation to frontDoor.startRun(body) with no bearer/token/Access check at all. POST /runs provisions a Cloudflare Sandbox, runs real Pi planner/worker/verifier lanes (burns Codex/Pi tokens = real money), and can drive leased GitHub branch+PR, Discord sends, Linear comments, and public Wzrrd publishes. The GET /runs/:runId/events and /runs/:runId/debugger read routes are likewise unauthenticated. The Worker is live and public (docs/workflow-app-spine.md:79 'https://pi-cloudflare-sandbox-workflows.joelhooks.workers.dev'), and the docs publicly paste working /runs/.../events and /debugger URLs. Nothing in the repo (wrangler.jsonc, worker.ts, docs) configures Cloudflare Access or any edge auth in front of /runs, so anyone who can reach the URL can spend the account's agent budget and read run event streams. Admin setup routes are protected while the actual expensive capability is wide open.

**Evidence:** if (url.pathname !== "/runs") { return jsonError(404, ...); }
 if (input.request.method !== "POST") { ... }
 // no auth check here
 const frontDoor = input.createFrontDoor?.(input.env) ?? createFrontDoorFromEnv(input.env);
 const result = WorkflowRunResultSchema.parse(await frontDoor.startRun(body));

**Fix:** Require a bearer token on /runs and the /runs/:runId/* read routes using the same timingSafeSecretMatch + a RUNS_ACCESS_TOKEN (or reuse WORKFLOW_APP_ADMIN_TOKEN) binding, or document and enforce a Cloudflare Access policy in wrangler config. Fail closed if the token binding is absent.

### [HIGH] [architecture] Capability plumbing is monomorphic: adding a sixth capability is shotgun surgery across every layer

**Where:** `src/app/application/ports.ts:129`

The capability concept (lease -> execute -> record receipt) is re-implemented per capability instead of abstracted once. ports.ts defines five structurally identical adapter interfaces (Discord, Wzrrd, GitHubPullRequest, GitHubBranchCommit, LinearComment), all shaped `execute({lease, payload}) => DeliveryResult`. CapabilityLeaseBrokerActorContract has five `record*Execution` methods; the broker implementation (src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-capability-lease-broker.ts:461-770) duplicates the same ~70-line body five times, differing only in capability string, schema name, and receiptKind. WorkflowApp duplicates a ~200-line lease/approve/execute/record pipeline per target (deliverLinearReviewSurface at 1997, deliverWzrrdReviewSurface at 2241, deliverGitHubReviewBranch at 2460, deliverGitHubReviewSurface at 2666), and WorkflowDependencies hardcodes per-capability fields plus per-capability secretRef objects (workflow-app.ts:114-154). The worker route env schema also carries per-capability secret-ref bindings. Adding one capability today means touching domain/schemas.ts, ports.ts (interface + broker method), the broker, workflow-app (new ~200-line method), worker-route env schema, and index.ts. This is the textbook shotgun-surgery smell and is the single biggest tax on the spine's stated goal of absorbing new workflow capabilities.

**Evidence:** ports.ts: `recordDiscordExecution(...)`, `recordGitHubPullRequestExecution(...)`, `recordGitHubBranchCommitExecution(...)`, `recordLinearCommentExecution(...)`, `recordWzrrdExecution(...)` all `(input: { delivery; lease }) => Promise<CapabilityLeaseReceipt>`; broker bodies are byte-near-identical: both recordDiscordExecution and recordGitHubBranchCommitExecution do `config.artifacts.writeJson({ path: `receipts/capability-executions/${safePathSegment(input.lease.leaseId)}.json`, ... })` then the same `update capability_leases set status = ? where lease_id = ?`.

**Fix:** Collapse to one generic `CapabilityAdapter<Payload, Delivery>` registry keyed by capability id (the discriminant already exists on CapabilityLease.capability), one generic `recordExecution(lease, delivery)` on the broker that dispatches schema validation via a capability descriptor table, and one parameterized review-surface delivery pipeline driven by `outputTarget.kind`. The five payload/delivery zod schemas stay; everything else becomes data.

### [HIGH] [architecture] workflow-app.ts is a 4,241-line god-file whose application layer embeds machine validation, per-target delivery, proof capture, and its own test doubles

**Where:** `src/app/application/workflow-app.ts:1115`

The entire use-case layer is one file: ~700 lines of free-function generated-machine validation (validateGeneratedMachineBinding/TransitionTargets/StepReferences/StartupProtocol/TerminalStates/StepTransitions/AgainstPlan, lines 576-836), Discord step rebinding (920-1083), the WorkflowApp class with a 356-line run() (1235-1591) plus a dozen 150-250 line private methods, and integration-test fakes inline: executeResearchReviewStep writes synthetic worker output via `this.dependencies.artifacts.writeJson` when `isIntegrationTestMode()` (line 3942+), and verification is bypassed in-core (line 3639: 'Integration-test path bypassed verifier execution'). Per Ousterhout this is the opposite of a deep module: the class interface is one method (`run`) but the implementation conflates at least four separable concerns, and the test-mode branches mean ports do not fully isolate the core — the core itself branches on execution mode instead of receiving fake adapters at the edge. This file will be the merge-conflict and comprehension bottleneck for every future change.

**Evidence:** grep of workflow-app.ts: `1235: async run(...)` ... `1591: }` (356 lines); `810:const validateGeneratedMachineAgainstPlan`; `3639: if (this.isIntegrationTestMode()) { ... "Integration-test path bypassed verifier execution..." }`; executeResearchReviewStep: `if (!this.isIntegrationTestMode()) { return { blocker: ... "Production research/review steps require a real worker agent lane." } } const writeReceipt = await this.dependencies.artifacts.writeJson({ path: input.step.outputPath, ... })`

**Fix:** Three extractions, in value order: (1) move the validateGeneratedMachine* family into a pure domain module (it only touches DynamicWorkflowMachineDocument/PlanDocument — it is domain logic, not application wiring); (2) extract review-surface delivery into one service parameterized by capability descriptor (merges with finding 1); (3) replace `executionMode: "integration-test"` branches with injected fake AgentWorkerLanePort/verifier adapters provided by tests/integration/workflow-app-fixtures.ts, so the core has exactly one behavior.

### [HIGH] [concurrency-durability] Capsule supervisor lane admission leaks slots permanently on crash — no expiry, no alarm, and 'deferred' is thrown as a hard error

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-capsule-supervisor.ts:119`

admitLane adds laneId to activeLaneIds; only releaseLane removes it. If the worker dies between admit and release (the window covers an entire Pi sandbox lane run), the laneId stays active forever: there is no admittedAt timestamp on the stored record, no DO alarm, no staleness eviction. Re-running the same runId then gets status 'deferred' (reason already-active) forever — and createAdmittedAgentLaneRuntime converts ANY deferred decision into a thrown Error (admitted-agent-lane-runtime.ts:81-85) with no retry loop, despite the decision advertising retryAfterSeconds. So one crash mid-lane permanently wedges that laneId, leaks one slot of maxActiveLanes for the workItemId DO (shared across all future runs of that work item), and after maxActiveLanes crashes the work item can never run another lane.

**Evidence:** cloudflare-capsule-supervisor.ts:119-133: `if (record.activeLaneIds.includes(input.laneId)) { return json(...{ reason: "already-active", retryAfterSeconds: 1, status: "deferred" }); }` — no expiry field, no alarm() anywhere in src/app. admitted-agent-lane-runtime.ts:81-85: `if (admission.status === "deferred") { throw new Error(\`Agent lane admission deferred: ... retry after ${admission.retryAfterSeconds}s.\`); }`

**Fix:** Store admittedAt per active lane, set a DO alarm to evict stale admissions past lane timeoutMs, and either honor retryAfterSeconds with a bounded retry loop or treat already-active-with-stale-admission as reclaimable.

### [HIGH] [concurrency-durability] Capability lease expiry and single-use status are never enforced; deterministic lease_id makes any same-runId retry crash after the side effect

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-capability-lease-broker.ts:437`

Leases are issued with expiresAt (workflow-app sets now+15min) and persisted with status 'issued', but the broker never reads the lease row back: grep confirms expires_at is only ever inserted, never selected, and recordXExecution runs an unconditional `update capability_leases set status='executed' where lease_id=?` with no `and status='issued' and expires_at > now` guard. The execution flow is requestLease → adapter.execute (real Discord/GitHub/Linear/Wzrrd side effect) → recordExecution. If the worker crashes between execute and recordExecution, the external side effect happened but the lease stays 'issued' with no receipt — and because lease_id is the deterministic `lease:${capability}:${runId}` (a D1 primary key), any retry of the same runId throws a raw PK-conflict exception inside requestLease's insert, so the run can never be completed or retried; conversely nothing in the code path would stop a re-executed in-memory lease from delivering the side effect a second time, since lease state in D1 is write-only audit, not enforcement.

**Evidence:** Lease id: `leaseId: \`lease:${input.request.capability}:${input.request.runId}\`` (line 437) inserted via plain `insert into capability_leases (...)` (lines 165-167, PK lease_id). Execution recording (lines 504-512): `update capability_leases set status = ? where lease_id = ?` bound to ("executed", leaseId) — no status/expiry predicate. Workflow flow (workflow-app.ts:2342-2402): `requestLease(... expiresAt: new Date(Date.now() + 15 * 60_000) ...)` then `await this.dependencies.wzrrdPublisher.execute(...)` then `recordWzrrdExecution(...)`.

**Fix:** Make requestLease idempotent (insert ... on conflict return existing lease, denying if status != 'issued' or expired), and gate execution recording with `where lease_id=? and status='issued' and expires_at > ?`, treating 0 rows changed as a replay signal that must not re-deliver.

### [HIGH] [concurrency-durability] Retrying a run with the same runId is guaranteed to fail, and concurrent same-runId runs silently overwrite each other's D1 events

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-workflow-status-projection.ts:59`

There is no run-level idempotency anywhere: the front door never checks the runs table for an existing run_id (grep shows no `select ... from runs` in src/app outside the read-only event-stream reader), the runs row is a blind upsert, and workflow_events rows are written with event_index = the in-memory eventLog.length with `on conflict(run_id, event_index) do update`. A client retry of POST /runs after a 500 (the route returns 500 'workflow_route_failed' on any mid-run error) re-executes from scratch with the same runId: it will burn a real planner agent lane until admitLane returns 'already-completed', which createAdmittedAgentLaneRuntime turns into a thrown error ('Agent lane already completed; refusing duplicate execution'), blocking the run — so transient failures are unrecoverable per runId by design but cost agent spend on every retry attempt. If two requests with the same runId run concurrently, their per-request event counters interleave and the upsert silently rewrites each other's event history and run status, corrupting the audit trail that the verifier and review surface treat as evidence.

**Evidence:** cloudflare-workflow-status-projection.ts:59 `event_index: projection.eventCount` (eventCount = in-memory `eventLog.length`, workflow-app.ts:1265) written via `insert into workflow_events ... on conflict(run_id, event_index) do update set ... state = excluded.state, summary = excluded.summary` (lines 97-107). admitted-agent-lane-runtime.ts:75-79: `if (admission.status === "already-completed") { throw new Error(\`Agent lane already completed; refusing duplicate execution: ${request.laneId}\`); }`

**Fix:** Reject (or short-circuit to the stored receipt) POST /runs whose run_id already exists in runs; alternatively make lanes idempotent by returning the persisted lane receipt on already-completed instead of throwing.

### [HIGH] [concurrency-durability] Parallel-workflow spike: admitLane grants a second slot to an already-running lane on queue redelivery — duplicate execution in the same sandboxId

**Where:** `prototypes/cloudflare-parallel-workflow-spike/src/worker.ts:186`

Unlike the production supervisor (which defers on activeLaneIds.includes), this spike's admitLane only checks committed/degraded status, machine state, and the cap count. A lane in status 'running' whose consumer crashed (or whose message was duplicated — Queues are at-least-once, and Pi lanes here run up to 300s inside the consumer) gets admitted AGAIN: activeLaneIds gets a duplicate entry, and buildSandboxId(runId, laneId, lane.retryCount) returns the SAME sandboxId because retryCount was not bumped (only fail-lane bumps it). Two consumers then exec concurrently in one sandbox (clobbering /workspace, double-charging compute), and the leaked first slot eats the concurrency cap; if lanes keep crashing before /fail-lane is recorded, slots accumulate until every admission returns concurrency-cap-full, messages exhaust queue retries to the DLQ, and the run wedges in runningFanoutLanes forever (no alarm/reaper in this DO either). The newer joelclaw-research-swarm-spike copied the same admitLane verbatim (worker.ts:151-208) including the missing already-active check.

**Evidence:** admitLane (lines 189-217): `if (lane.status === "committed" || lane.status === "degraded") { return json({ alreadyDone: true ... }) } ... if (record.activeLaneIds.length >= ...concurrencyCap) {...} const sandboxId = buildSandboxId(record.runId, body.laneId, lane.retryCount);` — no `record.activeLaneIds.includes(body.laneId)` or `lane.status === "running"` guard before `activeLaneIds: [...record.activeLaneIds, body.laneId]`.

**Fix:** Defer (or reclaim-after-staleness) when the lane is already in activeLaneIds / status running, and include an attempt nonce in sandboxId so a reclaimed lane never shares a sandbox with a zombie attempt.

### [HIGH] [concurrency-durability] Parallel-workflow spike: any crash during finalization wedges the run forever — redelivered finalize messages are acked as no-ops

**Where:** `prototypes/cloudflare-parallel-workflow-spike/src/worker.ts:250`

beginFinalization returns shouldFinalize:false whenever status is 'synthesizing' or 'verifying', with no check of whether synthesis/verification actually completed. First finalize attempt sets status 'synthesizing' then runs three sequential sandbox phases in the consumer; if the consumer crashes or any phase throws after that point, the queue redelivers the finalize message, begin-finalization answers shouldFinalize:false, processFinalizeMessage returns, and the message is acked — the run is permanently stuck in synthesizing/verifying with no reaper. A second variant of the same crash window: completeLane persists the fan-in-satisfied record and only THEN sends the finalize queue message (lines 296-308); a failure between putRecord and LANE_QUEUE.send means finalize is never enqueued and nothing re-triggers it. Notably the joelclaw-research-swarm-spike already fixed the first bug (its beginFinalization returns shouldFinalize:true when 'status === "synthesizing" && !record.synthesisCommitSha', etc., worker.ts:229-237) — that fix was never backported here, which is real drift if this spike is still a reference for the production spine.

**Evidence:** Lines 250-252: `if (record.status === "synthesizing" || record.status === "verifying") { return json({ ok: true, record, shouldFinalize: false }); }` — contrast joelclaw-research-swarm-spike/src/worker.ts:229-230: `if (record.status === "synthesizing" && !record.synthesisCommitSha) { return json({ ok: true, record, shouldFinalize: true }); }`. completeLane: `await this.putRecord(...); if (fanInSatisfied) { await this.env.LANE_QUEUE.send({ ... type: "finalize" ... }); }`

**Fix:** Backport the swarm spike's resumable beginFinalization checks (phase-completion shas) and enqueue the finalize message before (or atomically with) the state flip, since begin-finalization already dedupes.

### [HIGH] [correctness-infra] Capability lease broker uses non-idempotent plain INSERTs with deterministic IDs — retries or two same-capability steps in one run hit UNIQUE constraint failures

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-capability-lease-broker.ts:162`

leaseId is `lease:${capability}:${runId}` (line 437) and receipt ids are `${leaseId}:lease` / `${leaseId}:discord` etc. `persistIssuedLease` and `writeReceiptRow` do plain `insert into capability_leases ...` / `insert into receipts ...` with no `on conflict` clause, while `capability_leases.lease_id` and `receipts.receipt_id` are PRIMARY KEY in migrations/0001_workflow_app_d1.sql. Consequences: (a) any run whose generated plan contains two steps of the same capability (the planner is an LLM; uniqueness is only encouraged by prompt text) produces the same lease_id twice and the second `requestLease` blows up with a D1 UNIQUE-constraint error mid-run, after earlier side effects executed; (b) replaying/retrying a run with the same runId (the entire run executes in one Worker fetch invocation, see separate finding) fails the same way. Every other D1 writer in this codebase is idempotent: review-gate actor uses `on conflict(review_id)/on conflict(receipt_id) do update` (cloudflare-review-gate-actor.ts:67,107), the status projection and telemetry sink also upsert. The broker is the only non-idempotent writer.

**Evidence:** `insert into capability_leases (lease_id, run_id, capability, resource_ref, payload_hash, policy_id, status, expires_at, redacted) values (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)` (no on-conflict), and broker's writeReceiptRow: `insert into receipts (receipt_id, run_id, receipt_kind, artifact_ref, receipt_hash, redacted) values (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)` vs. review-gate actor's `insert into receipts ... on conflict(receipt_id) do update set ...`

**Fix:** Include the stepId in leaseId, and add `on conflict ... do update` to both broker insert statements to match the review-gate actor.

### [HIGH] [correctness-infra] Production front door executes the entire multi-lane agent run inside one Worker fetch invocation — no Cloudflare Workflows durability exists in the production path

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-workflow-front-door.ts:422`

`startRun` constructs the full WorkflowApp and `await workflow.run(request)` synchronously inside the `POST /runs` handler (invoked directly at cloudflare-worker-route.ts:1312 `await frontDoor.startRun(body)`). There is no `WorkflowEntrypoint`, no `step.do`, and wrangler.jsonc has no `workflows` binding — only Durable Objects. So planner lane + N worker lanes + verifier lane + capability executions (each a sandbox session with `timeoutMs` per lane) all run within a single request: an isolate eviction, deploy, uncaught exception, or client disconnect mid-run loses the run entirely, leaving partial non-idempotent D1 writes (leases/receipts) and orphaned run repos with no resume path. This directly contradicts the repo premise ("running Pi inside Cloudflare Sandboxes via durable workflows") and the D1 sink description ("while the control plane advances the run"). A maintainer reading 'production spine' will assume durability that is not there.

**Evidence:** cloudflare-workflow-front-door.ts: `route: "POST /runs", async startRun(input) { ... const workflow = new WorkflowApp({...}); return await workflow.run(request); }` — and `grep -rn "WorkflowEntrypoint|step.do" src/app` returns nothing; wrangler.jsonc contains only durable_objects bindings.

**Fix:** Move run execution into a Cloudflare Workflows entrypoint with idempotent steps (or at minimum a queue/DO-driven resumable loop), and have POST /runs enqueue and return 202.

### [HIGH] [correctness-spine] Generated machine with a final initial state passes all validators and produces a 'captured' run with zero steps executed (proven end-to-end)

**Where:** `src/app/application/workflow-app.ts:680`

validateGeneratedMachineStartupProtocol checks that the initial state has no stepId and has an on.NEXT transition to the first planned step, but never checks that the initial state is not a final state. A planner-emitted machine document with `initial: "done"` where the done final state carries `on: { NEXT: { target: <first step> } }` passes every check in validateGeneratedMachineAgainstPlan (the schema allows `on` on final states, and XState silently ignores transitions on final states). At runtime the actor is immediately done, `workflowActor.send({ type: "NEXT" })` is a no-op, and the first loop iteration in executeDynamicWorkflow sees status "done" and sets workflowCompleted=true. The post-loop guard only checks `!workflowCompleted` — it never asserts `completedStepIds.size === loadedPlan.steps.length`. I verified this end-to-end with the real WorkflowApp + in-memory adapters: wrapping the integration planner to set `initial: "done"` yielded `status: captured`, `completedStepIds: []`, `generatedStateSequence: ["done"]` — a full run receipt, review surface, and execution proof for a run that executed nothing. Downstream cloudflare-proof guards don't save you: `workerLaneReceipts.every(isRealAgentLaneReceipt)` is vacuously true on an empty array, and the verification contract is itself planner-authored. Since the planner is a stochastic LLM lane and this validation layer exists precisely to constrain its output, this is a real hole in the spine's core guarantee (fake success looking like real work).

**Evidence:** validateGeneratedMachineStartupProtocol: `if (initialState.meta.stepId !== undefined) { return blocker(...) } const initialTarget = initialState.on["NEXT"]?.target;` — no `initialState.type === "final"` check. executeDynamicWorkflow post-loop: `if (!workflowCompleted) { ... return { result, status: "blocked" }; }` — no completedStepIds count check. Probe output: `status: captured / completedStepIds: [] / generatedStateSequence: ["done"]`

**Fix:** Two-line fix, defense in depth: (1) in validateGeneratedMachineStartupProtocol, block when `initialState.type === "final"` (or when `machine.xstate.initial` is "done"/"blocked"); (2) after the loop in executeDynamicWorkflow, block when `completedStepIds.size !== input.loadedPlan.steps.length`. The second check closes the whole class of skip-to-done machines regardless of future validator gaps.

### [HIGH] [docs-drift] Entire dream-memory-fabric/cartridge production surface is undocumented; CONTEXT.md claims a single Workflow App context

**Where:** `CONTEXT.md:3`

CONTEXT.md states the repo has "one production-intended context today" and that "Production code lives under src/app/", and its Bounded Contexts table lists only domain/application/workflow/control-plane/infrastructure. The workflow-app-spine.md architecture table (lines 30-39) lists the same five boundaries plus worker.ts. Reality: there is a large second production surface — src/app/workflow-nodes/ (dream-memory-fabric.ts at 1,949 lines, dream-memory-fabric-schemas.ts at 959 lines, dream-generated-workflow-proof.ts, artifact-backed-cartridge-adapter.ts; ~3,400 lines), src/cartridges/ (dream-memory-fabric cartridge plus cloudflare-workflow-cartridges.ts), dream relay infrastructure (trusted-dream-memory-relay-server.ts 432 lines, cloudflare-dream-memory-fabric-relay.ts 440 lines, cloudflare-workflow-cartridge-installer.ts), six app:dream:* package.json scripts with scripts/*.ts entrypoints, and 11+ dream/cartridge integration tests in tests/integration. Grep for "cartridge", "workflow-nodes", or "app:dream" across README.md, CONTEXT.md, AGENTS.md, VISION.md, and docs/workflow-app-spine.md returns zero hits. A maintainer relying on the docs has no idea roughly a third of the production code exists, what its contracts are, or how to run it.

**Evidence:** CONTEXT.md: "This repo has one production-intended context today: **Workflow App** ... Production code lives under `src/app/`." vs `wc -l src/app/workflow-nodes/*.ts`: dream-memory-fabric.ts 1949, dream-memory-fabric-schemas.ts 959, dream-generated-workflow-proof.ts 284, artifact-backed-cartridge-adapter.ts 167; package.json: "app:dream:run": "tsx scripts/workflow-app-dream-run.ts"; grep for cartridge/workflow-nodes/app:dream in docs/ README.md CONTEXT.md AGENTS.md returned no matches.

**Fix:** Add a dream-memory-fabric/cartridge section to docs/workflow-app-spine.md and CONTEXT.md (or a dedicated doc) covering src/app/workflow-nodes, src/cartridges, the relay server, the app:dream:* commands, and the workflow.node.invoke step kind, and update the bounded-context tables.

### [HIGH] [prototypes-hygiene] Graduated prototypes violate their own delete/absorb rules — disposition needed for all 17

**Where:** `prototypes/README.md:3`

The repo's own policy (prototypes/README.md: 'Every directory here is disposable... It is deleted or rewritten after capture'; docs/production-vs-prototypes.md: prototype code may not 'stay alive after learning is captured without a deletion date') has fired for at least 7 prototypes whose capabilities now exist in src/app, yet all still read 'Status: active'. Concretely: capsule-supervisor-spike and capsule-do-spike say 'Delete after the real Durable Object supervisor is rewritten into src/' — wrangler.jsonc now deploys class_name CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor as a production DO. secret-lease-broker-spike says 'Delete after a real control-plane secret broker is rewritten into src/' — src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-capability-lease-broker.ts (821 lines) exists. review-gate-spike says 'Delete after the review gate shape is rewritten into src/' — src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-review-gate-actor.ts exists. machine-planner-spike says 'Delete after the production planner is rewritten into src/' — the spine planner is the Pi planner lane in src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts (Zod-schema-validated PlannerLaneBlueprintDocumentSchema), a different and superseding design. cloudflare-registry-workflow-os-spike is already marked 'Status: captured' with rule 'Delete or rewrite after the real Cloudflare registry proof is captured' — src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-package-registry.ts exists. Several of these have deployed Cloudflare Workers, queues, and secrets (GitHub App keys in cloud-capability-lease-broker-spike) that keep accumulating drift and live attack surface the longer the corpses ride in the wagon.

**Evidence:** capsule-do-spike/README.md: 'Delete after the real Durable Object supervisor is rewritten into `src/` and integrated with the real sandbox/artifacts/wzrrd route.' — vs wrangler.jsonc: '"durable_objects": ... "class_name": "CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor"'. cloudflare-registry-workflow-os-spike/README.md: 'Status: captured ... Delete or rewrite after the real Cloudflare registry proof is captured.' — vs src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-package-registry.ts (303 lines) existing.

**Fix:** Per-prototype disposition: DELETE (absorbed, condition fired): capsule-supervisor-spike, capsule-do-spike, integrated-capsule-run-spike, secret-lease-broker-spike, review-gate-spike, machine-planner-spike (capture pattern-library receipt to .brain/ first), cloudflare-registry-workflow-os-spike (tear down Worker+queue first), prototypes/_captures/ (empty dir). DELETE AFTER WORKER TEARDOWN: sandbox-workflow-spike + cloudflare-parallel-workflow-spike + cloud-capability-lease-broker-spike (revoke GitHub App secrets) + workflow-observability-spine-spike — these have deployed Workers/queues/secrets; sandbox-workflow-spike must go together with integrated-capsule-run-spike which calls its deployed endpoint. KEEP WITH DEADLINE (only home of a needed capability): joelclaw-research-swarm-spike (research-swarm use case not yet a cartridge), shitrat-dream-workflow-spike (TODO.md tracks unproved seams; partially absorbed into src/cartridges/dream-memory-fabric), registry-office-ui-spike (only UI over the registry), system-dream-review-ui (canonical report pattern per docs/source-map.md, seed for wzrrd.report.render node). DELETE (one-shot reports already published): shitrat-brain-proposal-ui, dream-sequence-vision-ui.

### [HIGH] [security] Registry prototype is open-by-default: isAuthorized returns true when ACCESS_TOKEN is unset

**Where:** `prototypes/cloudflare-registry-workflow-os-spike/src/worker.ts:1003`

Unlike its sibling prototypes (sandbox/parallel/joelclaw/observability/capsule), which fall back to a localhost-hostname check when ACCESS_TOKEN is unset (and therefore deny on a public *.workers.dev hostname), the registry prototype returns true unconditionally. The deploy script only puts the secret conditionally ('test -z "$ACCESS_TOKEN" || ... secret put ACCESS_TOKEN' in package.json:47), so if ACCESS_TOKEN is empty in the deploy environment the secret is never set and every route — including run-triggering routes — is fully public with no gate at all.

**Evidence:** function isAuthorized(request: Request, env: Env): boolean {
  if (!env.ACCESS_TOKEN) {
    return true;
  }
  return request.headers.get("authorization") === `Bearer ${env.ACCESS_TOKEN}`;
}

**Fix:** Mirror the other prototypes: fall back to a localhost-only allow (or deny entirely) when ACCESS_TOKEN is unset, and/or make the deploy script fail when ACCESS_TOKEN is missing instead of silently skipping the secret put.

### [HIGH] [tests] Core Sandbox Pi lane runtime has zero test coverage; its test hooks are exported but never imported

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts:411`

The 422-line module that actually runs Pi inside a Cloudflare Sandbox (creates sandbox, writes prompt, mounts packages, runs the embedded shell/node scripts, clones/commits/pushes the artifact repo, destroys the sandbox on failure) is the reason this repo exists, and no test imports it. The module even exports __cloudflareSandboxAgentLaneTestHooks ({ buildPiAgentLaneCommand }) 'for tests', but grep shows the only reference is the re-export in src/app/index.ts:183 — no test uses it. Worse, the file tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts exists and its name claims this coverage, but it only imports agent-lane-json-output.ts and agent-lane-package-mounts.ts (helper modules). A maintainer scanning the test directory would believe the sandbox runtime is covered when it is not. Regressions in command construction, env propagation, or the error-path sandbox destroy (line 401: destroySandbox in catch) would ship silently.

**Evidence:** src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts:420: `export const __cloudflareSandboxAgentLaneTestHooks = { buildPiAgentLaneCommand, };` — `grep -rn "__cloudflareSandboxAgentLaneTestHooks" src tests` matches only `src/app/index.ts:183`. Meanwhile tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts imports only `from "../../src/app/infrastructure/agent-lane-json-output.ts"` and `from "../../src/app/infrastructure/agent-lane-package-mounts.ts"` — never the sandbox lanes module it is named after.

**Fix:** At minimum, add unit tests for buildPiAgentLaneCommand via the already-exported test hooks (assert the exact command/env for planner vs worker lanes, secret handling, timeout). Rename the existing test file to match what it covers (agent-lane-helpers), and add a workerd-based test (vitest-pool-workers) or a recorded-fake Sandbox contract test for runCloudflareSandboxPiAgentLane including the destroy-on-error path.

### [HIGH] [tests] Durable Object concurrency admission (CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor) untested; every test re-implements its semantics in a hand-rolled fake

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-capsule-supervisor.ts:103`

The DO that enforces lane concurrency caps, duplicate-lane admission, already-completed detection, and release bookkeeping against ctx.storage (325 lines) has no test referencing it. admitted-agent-lane-runtime.test.ts builds its own fake controller (createController, lines 127-168) that fabricates admission decisions, and cloudflare-workflow-front-door.test.ts uses another fake (createAdmissionCapsuleController). So the tests prove the *runtime wrapper* honors decisions, but the component that actually makes the decisions — the cap check `record.activeLaneIds.length >= input.maxActiveLanes` (line 135), the dedup check on line 119, and the storage read/parse/put cycle (lines 268-273) — is exercised nowhere. This is the classic drift risk: the fakes encode what the authors believe the DO does. A bug in releaseLane (e.g. failing to remove a lane id, leaving the cap permanently full and deadlocking all runs) would pass the entire suite.

**Evidence:** `grep -rln "CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor\|capsule-supervisor" tests/` returns nothing. tests/integration/admitted-agent-lane-runtime.test.ts:136-154 fakes admission: `admitLane(input) { admissions.push(input); const decision = decisions.shift() ?? AgentLaneAdmissionDecisionSchema.parse({ ... status: "admitted" ... }); return Promise.resolve(decision); }` while the real logic lives untested at cloudflare-capsule-supervisor.ts:135: `if (record.activeLaneIds.length >= input.maxActiveLanes)`.

**Fix:** The DO's admit/release logic is pure record-transformation around ctx.storage; extract it to a pure function and test admit-at-cap, admit-duplicate, release-unknown-lane, and already-completed transitions directly, or run the DO under @cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers with real DO storage.

### [HIGH] [tests] POST /runs is unauthenticated and no test asserts any auth on the main run-submission route

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-worker-route.ts:1262`

The worker route handler accepts POST /runs from anyone: the handler goes straight from method check to body parsing to frontDoor.startRun with no token/Access check. The only auth-rejection tests in the 1302-line cloudflare-worker-route.test.ts cover the admin package-seed routes ('rejects package seed requests without the admin bearer token'). Starting a run provisions an artifact repo, spends Pi/sandbox compute, and can trigger leased capabilities — this is the most expensive and most outward-facing endpoint, and the test suite encodes that it requires nothing. The prototype workers gated everything behind ACCESS_TOKEN; the production worker has no equivalent and no test demanding one, so the gap is invisible to CI.

**Evidence:** cloudflare-worker-route.ts:1262-1280: `if (url.pathname !== "/runs") { return jsonError(404, ...); } if (input.request.method !== "POST") { ... } let body: unknown; try { body = WorkflowRunRequestSchema.parse(await parseJsonRequest(input.request)); ...` — no bearerToken() call on this path; bearerToken (line 236) is only consulted by the seed guard at line 452. Test list for the route includes only `"rejects package seed requests without the admin bearer token"` for auth.

**Fix:** Decide the auth story for /runs (worker-level bearer token or Cloudflare Access service token validation), implement it, and add tests: 401 without credentials, 401 with wrong token, 202/200 with valid token. If auth is intentionally delegated to Cloudflare Access at the edge, encode that as a documented invariant and test that the handler rejects when the Access-injected header is absent.

### [MEDIUM] [architecture] Dependency inversion break: app/infrastructure imports src/cartridges, which imports back into src/app

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-worker-route.ts:6`

The generic worker route (infrastructure spine) imports the installed-cartridge registry from src/cartridges, and src/cartridges imports app/infrastructure (package-seeder, cartridge-installer) and app/workflow-nodes. That is a bidirectional src/app <-> src/cartridges dependency: the spine knows about its plugins instead of being composed at the entrypoint (src/app/worker.ts, which is only 9 lines and would be the natural composition root). Worse, the route's env binding schema is literally aliased to the dream cartridge's schema (`InstalledWorkflowCartridgeEnvBindingSchema = DreamMemoryFabricCloudflareEnvBindingSchema` in cloudflare-workflow-cartridges.ts:13), so every installed cartridge's Cloudflare bindings leak into the generic route contract. Adding a second cartridge forces edits inside the spine's infrastructure layer rather than only in the cartridge and the composition root.

**Evidence:** cloudflare-worker-route.ts: `import { InstalledWorkflowCartridgeEnvBindingSchema, defaultPackageSeedTemplatesWithInstalledCartridges, workflowCartridgeDependenciesFromWorkerBindings } from "../../cartridges/cloudflare-workflow-cartridges.ts";` while cloudflare-workflow-cartridges.ts has `import { defaultPackageSeedTemplates } from "../app/infrastructure/cloudflare-package-seeder.ts";`

**Fix:** Make createWorkflowWorkerHandler accept a cartridge registry (installers + seed templates + env schema fragment) as a parameter with the current set as default supplied from worker.ts. src/cartridges may depend on src/app; src/app must never depend on src/cartridges.

### [MEDIUM] [architecture] workflow-nodes/ is an undocumented sixth layer holding a ~3,300-line vertical feature with ports and schemas outside their declared homes

**Where:** `src/app/workflow-nodes/dream-memory-fabric.ts:74`

src/README.md documents exactly five layers (domain, application, workflow, control-plane, infrastructure) and the rule 'Zod owns runtime contracts' under domain. workflow-nodes/ is none of these. dream-memory-fabric.ts (1,949 lines) defines its own ports (DreamMemoryFabricPort, DreamMemoryRetrievalPort, DreamMemoryBackfillPort) inside the feature file rather than application/ports.ts, and dream-memory-fabric-schemas.ts (959 lines) is a second schema home outside domain/. The app barrel (src/app/index.ts) then re-exports roughly 120 Dream* symbols (lines 55-173), so one feature dominates the package's public API. The layering decision may be fine (vertical slice behind WorkflowNodeAdapterPort), but it is currently invisible: a maintainer following the README will not find half the runtime contracts, and the next cartridge author has no documented pattern to copy.

**Evidence:** src/README.md lists only `app/domain/`, `app/application/`, `app/workflow/`, `app/control-plane/`, `app/infrastructure/` (no workflow-nodes); dream-memory-fabric.ts declares `export interface DreamMemoryFabricPort { inventorySources(...) ... }` outside application/ports.ts; wc -l: dream-memory-fabric.ts 1949, dream-memory-fabric-schemas.ts 959.

**Fix:** Either promote workflow-nodes to a documented layer with an explicit rule ('feature node adapters colocate their ports and schemas; the spine only sees WorkflowNodeAdapterPort'), or move the Dream ports into application/ports.ts and schemas into domain/. Also split dream-memory-fabric.ts — it is the second-largest file in the repo and covers inventory, hydration, search, correlation, backfill, HITL reporting, and refinement proposals in one module. Trim the Dream re-exports from index.ts to the surface cartridge consumers actually need.

### [MEDIUM] [architecture] AgentLaneRuntimeRequest port leaks sandbox/Pi runtime mechanics into the application boundary

**Where:** `src/app/application/ports.ts:244`

Cloudflare types do NOT leak into domain/application (verified: zero @cloudflare imports there; the lease broker even defines D1DatabaseLike structurally). But the agent-lane port leaks a different implementation: AgentLaneRuntimeRequest encodes the Cloudflare-sandbox shell-script contract — git artifact remote + token secret name, base64-encoded Pi auth file, prompt/receipt/transcript filesystem paths, and a hardcoded `provider: "openai-codex"` literal. Any alternative runtime (the README's 'future Think lanes', or a non-sandbox runtime) must impersonate this exact filesystem-and-git layout, and supporting a second Pi provider requires editing the port type itself. The port describes how the current adapter works, not what the application needs (a deep-module inversion: the abstraction is shaped by its only implementation).

**Evidence:** ports.ts AgentLaneRuntimeRequest: `readonly artifactRemote: string; readonly artifactTokenSecret: string; ... readonly leasedPiAuthJsonBase64: string; readonly prompt: string; readonly promptPath: string; readonly provider: "openai-codex"; readonly receiptPath: string; ... readonly transcriptPath: string;` consumed by cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts:360 `PI_PROVIDER: input.input.provider`.

**Fix:** Shrink the port to intent (laneId, kind, prompt, model, provider as a branded string, timeout, package mounts, trace context, auth lease) and let the sandbox runtime derive its own paths/remote/secret wiring from its config. At minimum widen `provider` to a domain enum so adding a provider is a schema change, not a port-type change.

### [MEDIUM] [concurrency-durability] Registry-workflow-os spike: queue jobs have zero idempotency — terminal jobs are resurrected and failed jobs retry-storm

**Where:** `prototypes/cloudflare-registry-workflow-os-spike/src/worker.ts:656`

processValidationJob/processInstallSmokeJob never consult job status before executing: a redelivered message (crash between /job-complete and message.ack, or any duplicate) re-runs the full sandbox validation and markJobRunning flips a 'succeeded'/'failed' job straight back to 'running' with no status guard (lines 300-317). Worse, on failure the consumer records the terminal failure via /job-failed and then rethrows, so the outer handler calls message.retry — every transient error therefore re-executes a job whose record already says 'failed', flapping validationStatus failed→running→failed until queue max retries, with each attempt reusing the same deterministic sandboxId (buildSandboxId('rv', message.name)) so concurrent duplicates share one sandbox.

**Evidence:** Lines 656-662: `await postDo(stub, "/job-failed", { error: ..., jobId: message.jobId, ... }); throw error;` followed by the queue handler's `catch (error) { ... message.retry({ delaySeconds: QUEUE_RETRY_DELAY_SECONDS }); }` (lines 596-599). markJobRunning (lines 300-317) sets `status: "running"` after only `requireValue(record.jobs[body.jobId], "job")` — no check of current status.

**Fix:** Add an admit-style gate: refuse to run a job whose status is succeeded/failed (ack the duplicate), and decide retry vs. terminal-fail inside the DO (like the lane spikes' fail-lane retryCount) instead of recording failure and retrying anyway.

### [MEDIUM] [concurrency-durability] Capsule supervisor stores all events and lane receipts for a work item in a single DO storage value — unbounded growth toward the 128 KiB value cap

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-capsule-supervisor.ts:178`

The DO keeps one 'record' key per workItemId containing every workflow event ever appended (appendEvent pushes onto record.events with no pruning), plus completedLaneIds, failedLaneIds, and a laneReleases map that grows with every lane of every run. The DO is keyed by workItemId, not runId, so this accumulates across all runs of a work item for its lifetime. Durable Object storage values are capped at 128 KiB; each run appends ~15-20 events (each with summary + refs hashes) plus 3+ lane release receipts, so a long-lived work item will eventually make putRecord throw on every appendEvent — and since workflow-app awaits appendEvent inside every transition, the work item becomes permanently unable to run anything (every run blocks at the first transition).

**Evidence:** appendEvent (lines 178-190): `await this.putRecord(SupervisorRecordSchema.parse({ ...record, events: [...record.events, input.event], ... }))` into the single key: `await this.ctx.storage.put("record", ...)` (line 273), with `events: z.array(WorkflowEventSchema).default([])` and `laneReleases: z.record(...)` in SupervisorRecordSchema (lines 38-49) and no deletion/truncation anywhere in the class.

**Fix:** Shard events into per-run keys (or stop mirroring events in the DO at all — D1 workflow_events already holds them) and prune lane bookkeeping for completed runs.

### [MEDIUM] [correctness-infra] Package registry parse throws on NULL manifest_hash rows — the null-stripping conditional is dead code

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-package-registry.ts:109`

`parsePackageRow` builds `manifestHash = row.manifest_hash === null ? {} : {manifest_hash: ...}` then does `D1PackageRowSchema.parse({ ...row, ...manifestHash })`. Spreading `{}` over `...row` does NOT remove the `manifest_hash: null` key already present from the row spread, and `D1PackageRowSchema.manifest_hash` is `Sha256HexSchema.optional()` which rejects null. Verified with the repo's zod: `{...{id:'x',manifest_hash:null}, ...{}}` => `{"id":"x","manifest_hash":null}` => `safeParse(...).success === false`. The `packages.manifest_hash` column is nullable in both d1-schema.ts and migrations/0001 (`manifest_hash text`), so any row inserted without a hash makes `discoverMetadata()` (which maps every entitled row) throw a ZodError — one bad row blocks planning for every run by that actor. The event-stream reader got the same problem right by building the object key-by-key (cloudflare-workflow-event-stream.ts:36-52); the registry got it wrong.

**Evidence:** const manifestHash = row["manifest_hash"] === null || row["manifest_hash"] === undefined ? {} : { manifest_hash: row["manifest_hash"] }; return D1PackageRowSchema.parse({ ...row, ...manifestHash });

**Fix:** Destructure manifest_hash out of the row before spreading: `const { manifest_hash, ...rest } = row; return D1PackageRowSchema.parse({ ...rest, ...manifestHash });` and add a test with a NULL manifest_hash row.

### [MEDIUM] [correctness-spine] Thrown adapter errors escape the safety envelope, leaving the run permanently non-terminal in persistent state

**Where:** `src/app/application/workflow-app.ts:1283`

Error handling in run() is inconsistent: packageRegistry.discoverMetadata, the planner, verifiers, and workflowNodeAdapter are wrapped in try/catch and converted to BLOCK transitions, but many other awaited dependencies are not — contextCapsules.resolve, every artifacts.writeJson/writeText in run()/pinDynamicWorkflowBlueprint (plan pin, machine/harness/contract writes), observabilityRecorder.capturePack, reviewGate.summarize, reviewSurfacePublisher.publish, capabilityLeases.record*Execution, and statusProjection.record inside transition() itself. Any rejection there throws straight out of run(). The front door (createCloudflareWorkflowFrontDoor.startRun) has no catch; the worker route catches and returns a 500 whose own message admits the problem: "Workflow route failed before a receipt was captured." The consequence is durable-state inconsistency: the D1 status projection and workflow_events log are left at a non-terminal state (e.g. executingDynamicWorkflow) with no BLOCK event, no blocker, no receipt. The SSE event-stream tail in cloudflare-worker-route.ts only stops on terminal states (`terminalWorkflowStates = new Set(["blocked", "captured"])`), so consumers poll until the budget runs out. For a system whose entire observability story is the safety-envelope event log, a mid-run R2/D1/network failure produces a run that looks forever in-flight.

**Evidence:** run(): `const capsule = await this.dependencies.contextCapsules.resolve({ runId: request.runId, workItemId: request.workItemId });` (no try/catch), `const planWrite = await this.dependencies.artifacts.writeJson({ path: "run/plan.json", ... })` (no try/catch); cloudflare-worker-route.ts:1327-1331: `return jsonError(500, "workflow_route_failed", "Workflow route failed before a receipt was captured.");`

**Fix:** Wrap the body of run() (after actor.start()) in a top-level try/catch that converts unexpected throws into `block(blocker("adapter_unavailable", ...), ...)` so the envelope machine and status projection always reach a terminal state — with a best-effort fallback if the projection write itself is what failed.

### [MEDIUM] [correctness-spine] Capability lease identity is keyed by capability+runId, so two steps with the same capability in one plan collide (duplicate-PK throw in prod, silent receipt overwrite in tests)

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-capability-lease-broker.ts:437`

Both brokers mint `leaseId: lease:${capability}:${runId}` with no per-step component, and DynamicWorkflowStepSchema/the machine validators happily accept a plan containing two `capability.discord.message` steps (both bound to the same pinned payload). In production, capability_leases has `lease_id text primary key` (migrations/0001_workflow_app_d1.sql:40), so the second requestLease for the same capability fails the D1 insert and assertD1Write throws — un-caught by the discord branch of executeDynamicWorkflow (`const leaseDecision = await this.requestDiscordLease(...)` has no try/catch), so the run dies via finding #2's unhandled-throw path after the first Discord message was already sent. In the in-memory policy broker the same plan instead 'succeeds' while writing every receipt to the fixed path `receipts/discord-capability.json`, so the second receipt artifact overwrites the first and both CapabilityLeaseReceipt.receiptRef values point at one artifact describing only the last delivery — corrupting the audit trail the receipts exist to provide. Same fixed-path pattern applies to the other capabilities in the memory broker. Integration tests therefore cannot detect the production-breaking duplicate-lease behavior.

**Evidence:** cloudflare-capability-lease-broker.ts: `leaseId: ``lease:${input.request.capability}:${input.request.runId}``` and `insert into capability_leases (lease_id, ...)` with `lease_id text primary key`; memory-adapters.ts recordDiscordExecution: `const writeReceipt = await artifactStore.writeJson({ path: "receipts/discord-capability.json", ... })` and `leaseId: ``lease:${input.capability}:${input.runId}```

**Fix:** Include the step id (or a monotonically increasing lease sequence) in leaseId and in receipt paths — workflow-app already passes a per-step traceContext (`stepId`) into the lease request, so the broker has the data. Alternatively, validate at plan-load time that each leased capability appears at most once per run if that is the intended invariant.

### [MEDIUM] [docs-drift] workflow-app-spine.md claims generated machine source and harness are optional; the schema and loader make both mandatory

**Where:** `docs/workflow-app-spine.md:100`

The spine doc repeatedly describes the generated machine TypeScript source and the harness artifact as conditional: "optional generated machine TypeScript source for inspection", "generated harness artifact ref and hash, only when the planner creates new step bindings or adapter wiring" (line 126), "Generated TypeScript source may be emitted as an inspectable/debuggable artifact, but it is not required for execution" (line 112), and the integration-assertions section says machine.ts/harness.ts are written only "when ... emitted"/"when new step bindings ... require it" (lines 428-429, 446-447). In code, DynamicWorkflowMachineArtifactSchema requires sourceArtifactRef/sourceHash, DynamicWorkflowPlanDocumentSchema requires harness: GeneratedHarnessArtifactSchema (no .optional()), and WorkflowApp.loadPinnedDynamicWorkflowMachine blocks the run if the machine source cannot be read or its hash mismatches — so the source is required for execution to proceed, not optional inspection material. CONTEXT.md (line 67) actually agrees with the code: "The app pins both `workflows/machine.config.json` and generated `workflows/machine.ts` before execution" — so the two docs also contradict each other.

**Evidence:** schemas.ts:756-762: `DynamicWorkflowMachineArtifactSchema = z.object({ artifactRef: ArtifactRefSchema, hash: Sha256HexSchema, machineId: ..., sourceArtifactRef: ArtifactRefSchema, sourceHash: Sha256HexSchema })`; schemas.ts:1070-1076: `DynamicWorkflowPlanDocumentSchema = DynamicWorkflowPlanBaseSchema.extend({ harness: GeneratedHarnessArtifactSchema, machine: ... })`; workflow-app.ts loadPinnedDynamicWorkflowMachine: `machineSource = await this.dependencies.artifacts.readText({ artifactRef: input.loadedPlan.machine.sourceArtifactRef })` with catch returning `status: "blocked"` and `if (sha256Hex(machineSource) !== input.loadedPlan.machine.sourceHash) ... blocked`.

**Fix:** Either make the schema fields optional to match the documented intent, or fix the spine doc to say machine source and harness are always pinned and hash-verified before execution (matching CONTEXT.md).

### [MEDIUM] [docs-drift] production-vs-prototypes.md says the capsule Durable Object is "not yet deployed as the production Worker path" — it is

**Where:** `docs/production-vs-prototypes.md:66`

The "Current prototype receipts" section still frames seam 1 (capsule keyed by external work item) as only partially proved by prototypes and explicitly says the DO capsule "is not yet deployed as the production Worker path." Reality: src/app/worker.ts exports CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor, wrangler.jsonc binds it as a Durable Object class, and docs/workflow-app-spine.md records multiple live deployed runs through that path (Worker https://pi-cloudflare-sandbox-workflows.joelhooks.workers.dev, e.g. run-live-verifier-lane-260609t1216-9078d6 with status captured). The doc describes the prototype era as the current state of the spine, which would misdirect a maintainer deciding what still needs to be built.

**Evidence:** production-vs-prototypes.md: "`capsule-do-spike/` proves the same boundary in local Wrangler ... It is not yet deployed as the production Worker path." vs src/app/worker.ts:6: `export { CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor } from "./infrastructure/cloudflare-capsule-supervisor.ts";` and wrangler.jsonc:38: `"class_name": "CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor"` plus spine doc line 47: "`CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor` is the production-intended Durable Object adapter for the context capsule and lane admission contracts."

**Fix:** Update the seam-status section to record that seams 1 and (partially) 3 now have deployed production-Worker receipts, pointing at docs/workflow-app-spine.md for the live receipt trail.

