### [MEDIUM] [security] Several prototype workers accept the access token as a URL query parameter

**Where:** `prototypes/sandbox-workflow-spike/src/worker.ts:1397`

sandbox-workflow-spike, cloudflare-parallel-workflow-spike, joelclaw-research-swarm-spike, and workflow-observability-spine-spike all accept the access token via ?token= in the URL in addition to the Authorization header. Tokens in URLs leak into Cloudflare/access logs, Referer headers when the page links out, browser history, and any intermediary that records request lines. Because this same token authorizes run-triggering routes that spend agent budget, a leaked URL token is a usable credential.

**Evidence:** const queryToken = url.searchParams.get("token");
 const headerToken = request.headers.get("X-Access-Token");
 const authorization = request.headers.get("Authorization");
 ...
 return [queryToken, headerToken, bearerToken].some((token) => token === env.ACCESS_TOKEN);

**Fix:** Drop the query-parameter token path; require the header form only. If a query token is genuinely needed for SSE/WebSocket clients that cannot set headers, scope it to read-only event routes and prefer a short-lived signed token.

### [MEDIUM] [security] Per-run Artifacts write credential is injected into the agent sandbox environment in cleartext

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts:330`

runCloudflareSandboxPiAgentLane builds ARTIFACTS_GIT_REMOTE as https://x:TOKEN@host (toAuthenticatedRemote) and passes it as a plain env var into the sandbox that executes the Pi agent. The Pi lane runs model-generated code with the artifact write token readable in its environment (env, /proc/self/environ, git config), so a misbehaving or prompt-injected agent can read the credential and push arbitrary content to the run's Artifacts repo or attempt to exfiltrate it. The token is per-run and time-scoped, which limits blast radius, but it is still a live write credential handed directly to untrusted agent code rather than mediated behind a helper that the agent cannot read.

**Evidence:** ARTIFACTS_GIT_REMOTE: toAuthenticatedRemote(input.input.artifactRemote, input.input.artifactTokenSecret).remote,
 ...
 remote: `https://x:${tokenSecret}@${remote.slice("https://".length)}`,

**Fix:** Confirm the lane truly needs git-push from inside the sandbox; if so, prefer a git credential helper or askpass that resolves the token out-of-band, scope the token to the single run repo (already partly done), and ensure it expires at lane teardown. Document this exposure as an accepted trust boundary if it must stay.

### [MEDIUM] [tests] No test touches a Workers runtime: all D1 is string-routed fakes, so SQL never executes and schema drift cannot fail

**Where:** `tests/integration/cloudflare-workflow-event-stream.test.ts:33`

Despite the directory being named tests/integration and the files being named cloudflare-*, the entire 156-test suite completes in ~680ms with zero workerd/miniflare involvement (no @cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers in devDependencies; wrangler is present but unused by tests). Every D1 interaction is a hand-rolled fake whose prepare() routes responses by substring matching on the SQL text and whose run() unconditionally returns success. The SQL in the adapters is never parsed or executed, so a typo'd column name, a query that violates the real migrations in migrations/0001-0003, or a D1 constraint error would pass all tests and only surface in production. D1 *failure* behavior (run() rejecting) is also untested anywhere. The fakes' row shapes happen to match migrations today (I checked workflow_events), but nothing enforces that they keep matching.

**Evidence:** tests/integration/cloudflare-workflow-event-stream.test.ts:33-39: `if (query.includes("from runs")) { return Promise.resolve({ results: input.runRows }); } if (query.includes("from workflow_events")) { return Promise.resolve({ results: input.eventRows }); }` and cloudflare-capability-lease-broker.test.ts:48-52: `run() { operations.push({ query, values }); return Promise.resolve({ success: true }); }`. package.json devDependencies contain vitest and wrangler but no @cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers.

**Fix:** Add a small second tier using @cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers (or miniflare's D1 over better-sqlite3) that applies migrations/*.sql and runs the event-stream reader, status projection, lease broker, and package registry against real SQLite. Keep the fast fakes for orchestration tests, but let at least one test per adapter execute its actual SQL.

### [MEDIUM] [tests] Production-mode safety guards in WorkflowApp are never asserted; the one production-mode test satisfies the 'Cloudflare execution proof' via a static constant

**Where:** `src/app/application/workflow-app.ts:1355`

17 of 18 WorkflowApp constructions in tests use executionMode: "integration-test", which bypasses verifier execution (VERIFICATION_BYPASSED), fabricates worker-lane receipts (shouldRecordIntegrationTestWorkerReceipts), and substitutes a local-integration execution proof. The production-only guards — block when the planner lane is not a realAgent (line 1355), block when deterministic/agent verifiers are missing (lines 3639/3663), block research/review steps without a real worker lane (line 3942), require a cloudflare-workers runtimeEnvironment for execution proof (line 1676) — have zero tests asserting they fire. A regression inverting any of these conditions (the rails that stop fake receipts reaching production) would not fail CI. The single production-mode test (front-door composition) passes while running in Node because the front door statically claims `platform: "cloudflare-workers"`, which also demonstrates the execution-proof requirement is satisfiable by assertion rather than evidence — the test proves the proof mechanism is self-certifying.

**Evidence:** workflow-app.ts:1354-1357: `!this.isIntegrationTestMode() && (!blueprint.plannerLane.realAgent || blueprint.plannerLane.runtime === "integration-test")` — `grep -rn executionMode tests/integration` shows every construction is `executionMode: "integration-test"` except cloudflare-workflow-front-door.test.ts, and cloudflare-workflow-front-door.ts:560-563 hardcodes `runtimeEnvironment: { platform: "cloudflare-workers", workerName: "pi-cloudflare-sandbox-workflows" }` even when invoked from a vitest Node process.

**Fix:** Add negative tests that construct WorkflowApp with executionMode "production" (or omitted) and assert each adapter_unavailable blocker fires: non-real planner lane, missing verifier, missing worker lane, missing runtimeEnvironment. Separately, consider deriving runtimeEnvironment from an actual runtime probe (e.g. navigator.userAgent === 'Cloudflare-Workers') instead of a constant, so the proof means something.

### [MEDIUM] [tests] Cloudflare Artifacts git store: real clone/commit/push paths untested; only ref-name derivation is covered

**Where:** `tests/integration/cloudflare-artifacts-store.test.ts:8`

Every artifact write/read in production flows through createCloudflareArtifactsGitStore, which does isomorphic-git clone/commit/push against the Artifacts remote (cloudflare-artifacts-store.ts lines 168-290: readJson, writeJson, push, clone by commitSha). The test file covers only namespace/artifactRef string derivation across three cases — never a write, read, commit, push, push-rejection, or token-expiry path. The supporting GitMemoryFS gets exactly two trivial assertions (ENOENT rejection and git init writes HEAD), nowhere near the surface isomorphic-git exercises during clone/commit/push. Since all other tests substitute createMemoryArtifactStore, the suite proves artifact *semantics* on a Map, while the component that persists every receipt, plan, and proof in production has its I/O entirely unverified.

**Evidence:** tests/integration/cloudflare-artifacts-store.test.ts asserts only `runStore.store.artifactRef({...})` string outputs (e.g. line 32: `artifactRef: "artifact://piwf-run-live-test/runs/run-live-test/run/planner-blueprint.json"`), while cloudflare-artifacts-store.ts imports `clone, commit as gitCommit, push` from isomorphic-git and uses them at lines 236 (`await push({...})`) and 277 (`await clone({...})`). git-memory-fs.test.ts is 29 lines with two assertions.

**Fix:** isomorphic-git works against a local bare repo over the filesystem or a local HTTP git server: add a round-trip test (writeJson → readJson → clone at commitSha) plus a push-failure test. This needs no Cloudflare account.

### [LOW] [architecture] Vocabulary drift: 'cartridge' is load-bearing in code but absent from the documented domain language

**Where:** `src/README.md:23`

The README declares the domain vocabulary: 'Package is the top-level saved primitive' with categories 'Context packs, workflow packs, tool packs, support packs, adapters, and kernels.' Cartridge is not in that list, yet it names a directory (src/cartridges/), domain schemas (WorkflowCartridgeManifestSchema, WorkflowCartridgeInvocationProofDocumentSchema at domain/schemas.ts:339-371), an installer, and an adapter — and `grep -rn cartridge docs/*.md` returns zero hits across all 13 docs. Meanwhile the same artifact is called a 'package' in proofs (packageId, pinnedPackage, manifestHash) and a 'workflow node' at invocation time. Three names for one lifecycle makes the planner contract, the seed templates, and the proof documents harder to correlate, and the next contributor will coin a fourth.

**Evidence:** src/README.md:23-24 lists package categories with no 'cartridge'; domain/schemas.ts:353 `WorkflowCartridgeManifestSchema`; docs/*.md contain 0 occurrences of 'cartridge'.

**Fix:** Pick one: either 'cartridge' is the official name for a package that exports workflow nodes — then add it to the README domain rules and docs — or rename the cartridge schemas/dirs to the package vocabulary. One sentence in src/README.md defining cartridge = workflow-node-exporting package would resolve most of it.

### [LOW] [correctness-infra] GitHub branch-commit adapter does not catch fetch network errors, unlike the pull-request adapter

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-github-branch-commit-adapter.ts:251`

The PR adapter wraps its fetch in try/catch and converts network failures into a blocked delivery (`catch { return blockerForGitHubStatus(503); }`, cloudflare-github-pull-request-adapter.ts:286-288). The branch-commit adapter makes six sequential fetch calls (`headRefResponse`, parent commit, tree, commit, ref write) with no try/catch, so a transient TCP/DNS failure throws out of `execute()` instead of returning a `blocked` GitHubBranchCommitDeliveryResult. Because the run executes in a single Worker invocation with the workflow-app expecting a delivery result (it handles `delivery.status === "blocked"` gracefully), an uncaught throw aborts the whole run instead of recording a blocker receipt. Worse, a network failure between the create-commit POST and the ref-write PATCH leaves an orphaned commit and an unrecorded partial state.

**Evidence:** const headRefResponse = await request(`${apiBase}/git/ref/heads/${encodedHeadBranch}`, { headers, method: "GET" }); — no try/catch anywhere in executeGitHubBranchCommit, vs. PR adapter: `try { response = await (config.fetch ?? fetch)(...); } catch { return blockerForGitHubStatus(503); }`

**Fix:** Wrap executeGitHubBranchCommit's fetches in try/catch returning blocked("adapter_unavailable", ...), matching the PR adapter.

### [LOW] [correctness-infra] Branch-commit dry-run/committed branchUrl is broken for github:repo: prefixed repository refs

**Where:** `src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-github-branch-commit-adapter.ts:111`

`repositoryParts` explicitly accepts refs of the form `github:repo:owner/repo` (and the blocked message documents both forms), but `branchUrlFor` interpolates the raw `repositoryRef` into the URL, producing `https://github.com/github:repo:owner/repo/tree/...` for the prefixed form. The PR adapter's deliveryForDryRun strips the prefix before building its compare URL (cloudflare-github-pull-request-adapter.ts:105-109); the branch-commit adapter forgot to. Receipts and review surfaces will carry dead links for the prefixed ref form.

**Evidence:** const branchUrlFor = (input) => `https://github.com/${input.repositoryRef}/tree/${encodeURIComponent(input.headBranch)}`; — while repositoryParts handles `repositoryRef.startsWith("github:repo:")`.

**Fix:** Normalize the ref (strip `github:repo:`) in branchUrlFor, as the PR adapter does.

### [LOW] [docs-drift] Safety-envelope state diagram omits the verifyingDynamicWorkflow state

**Where:** `docs/workflow-app-spine.md:271`

The "Safety Envelope States" section presents the current machine flow as executingDynamicWorkflow -> recordingReceipts (via requestingCapabilityLease/executingCapability) with no verification state. Both SafetyEnvelopeStateSchema and the actual machine in src/app/workflow/machine.ts include `verifyingDynamicWorkflow`, which sits between dynamic-workflow execution and recordingReceipts and handles DYNAMIC_WORKFLOW_VERIFIED / VERIFICATION_BYPASSED / BLOCK. Since verification is the trust core of this app, the canonical state diagram omitting the verification state is misleading.

**Evidence:** docs/workflow-app-spine.md lines 271-290 list received ... executingDynamicWorkflow -> recordingReceipts ... captured with no verifying state, while schemas.ts:1522 includes `"verifyingDynamicWorkflow"` in SafetyEnvelopeStateSchema and machine.ts:216 defines `verifyingDynamicWorkflow: { on: { DYNAMIC_WORKFLOW_VERIFIED: { target: "recordingReceipts" }, VERIFICATION_BYPASSED: ... } }`.

**Fix:** Insert verifyingDynamicWorkflow into the documented state flow between executingDynamicWorkflow and recordingReceipts.

